course: world
lesson: 86
title: Building strong things: bridges, towers and shapes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Builders think about two kinds of force inside a structure. Compression squashes a part, like the legs of a table under a heavy load. Tension stretches a part, like a rope holding up a swing. Stone and concrete are very strong when squashed but crack easily when stretched; steel is strong in both ways.
explain: The triangle is the strongest simple shape for a frame. Push on the corner of a square frame made of sticks and it folds into a slanted shape, but a triangle cannot change its shape unless a side breaks. That is why cranes, roofs, pylons and the Eiffel Tower are full of triangles.
explain: Bridges come in different kinds. A beam bridge is a flat deck resting on supports. An arch bridge carries the load around its curve and down into the ground on each side, so every stone is squeezed together. A suspension bridge hangs its road from strong steel cables slung between tall towers.
explain: Shape can make a weak material strong. A flat sheet of paper flops, but folded into a zigzag or rolled into a tube it can hold up a book. Hollow tubes are strong and light, which is why bicycle frames and the bones of birds are hollow.
explain: Tall things stay up when they are stable: a wide base and most of the weight low down make them hard to tip over. Builders also dig deep foundations, so a tall tower does not sink into the ground or lean.
fact: Compression is a force that squashes; tension is a force that stretches.
fact: Stone, brick, concrete and glass are strong in compression but weak in tension.
fact: When a plank or beam bends under a load, its top is squashed and its bottom is stretched.
fact: Steel is strong in both compression and tension.
fact: Reinforced concrete has steel bars inside it so it can resist stretching as well as squashing.
fact: A triangle frame keeps its shape when pushed, but a square frame can be pushed out of shape.
fact: Adding a diagonal strut to a square frame divides it into two triangles and makes it rigid.
fact: An arch carries its load down and outwards into the ground at each end.
fact: A suspension bridge hangs its road from steel cables held up by tall towers, so it can cross a wide river with few supports.
fact: Folding or rolling a sheet of paper makes it much stiffer.
fact: Hollow tubes are strong and light for their weight.
fact: An object with a wide base and most of its weight low down is hard to tip over.
fact: Foundations spread a building's weight into the ground.
exercise: What kind of force squashes a part of a structure? | Compression | Tension | Friction | Air resistance
exercise: A rope holds up a heavy swing. What kind of force is in the rope? | Tension | Compression | Friction | Upthrust
exercise: Why are cranes and electricity pylons built from many triangles? | A triangle frame cannot be pushed out of shape | Triangles use much more metal than squares | Triangles look better than other shapes | Triangles let the wind blow them over easily
exercise: A square frame of sticks wobbles. What is the best way to make it rigid? | Add a diagonal stick from corner to corner | Make all four sticks a little longer and thicker | Paint the whole frame with a thick coat of paint | Take away one of the sticks so it is lighter
exercise: Why are steel bars put inside concrete beams? | Concrete is weak when it is stretched | Steel makes the concrete set much faster | Steel bars make the beam lighter | Concrete is weak when it is squashed
exercise: How does a stone arch bridge carry its load? | It pushes the load down and outwards into the ground | It hangs the load from cables above it | It floats the load on the river below it | It pulls the load up into its middle stone
exercise: What holds up the road of a suspension bridge? | Steel cables hung from tall towers | Many stone arches under the water | A thick wooden beam across the river | Large air balloons under the deck
exercise: Which is the simplest kind of bridge, a flat deck resting on supports? | A beam bridge | A suspension bridge | An arch bridge | A cable bridge
exercise: How can a flat sheet of paper be made to hold up a book? | Fold it into a zigzag or roll it into a tube | Cut it into small squares and stack them in a pile | Make it wet so it gets heavier | Lay it flat on a smooth table
exercise: Why are the bones of birds hollow? | Hollow bones are strong but light for flying | Hollow bones break more easily when the bird lands | Hollow bones keep birds warm in the cold | Hollow bones store food for long flights
exercise: Which is hardest to tip over? | A box with a wide base and heavy things at the bottom | A tall box with heavy things at the top | A narrow box with heavy things at the top | A tall thin box standing on one corner
exercise: Why do tall buildings need deep foundations? | To spread their weight so they do not sink or lean | To make the building look taller | To store water for the people inside | To catch lightning before it can hit the roof at the top
exercise: Why is a bicycle frame made of tubes rather than solid bars? | Tubes are strong but much lighter | Tubes are cheaper because they bend easily | Tubes let air flow through the frame | Solid bars rust much faster than tubes
exercise: Which material is strong in both squashing and stretching? | Steel | Brick | Stone | Glass
test: You build a tower from spaghetti and marshmallows. It keeps leaning over. What will help most? | Adding diagonal pieces to make triangles | Using lots more marshmallows at the very top | Making the tower much thinner and much taller | Putting all the heaviest pieces at the top
test: Why do builders of old stone churches use arches over doors and windows? | Stone is strong when squashed, and an arch squeezes its stones | Arches use more stone, so they look grander | Stone is strong when stretched, and an arch stretches it | Arches let more rain run off the walls
test: A plank is laid across a stream and a child stands in the middle. Which is true? | The top of the plank is squashed and the bottom stretched | The whole plank is only stretched | The whole plank is only squashed | No forces act inside the plank at all
test: Why does a cardboard box get stronger when you add a corrugated layer, a wavy folded sheet, inside its walls? | The wavy folds make the card much stiffer | The folds make the card much heavier and softer | The folds glue the box shut | The folds keep the box dry
test: A hiker packs a rucksack. To stay steady on a narrow path, where should the heaviest things go? | Low down and close to the back | At the very top of the bag | In an outside pocket on one side | Hanging from a strap below the bag
test: Engineers want a long, light bridge across a wide river where few supports can stand. Which kind is best? | A suspension bridge | A beam bridge on many posts | A low stone arch bridge | A floating bridge of planks
test: Why would glass be a poor material for the cables of a suspension bridge? | Glass is weak when it is stretched | Glass is far too heavy to lift up there | Glass is weak when it is squashed | Glass rusts quickly in rain
test: A table wobbles when pushed sideways. A carpenter adds a slanted brace between a leg and the top. Why does it help? | It makes a triangle that cannot change shape | It makes the table much heavier, so it cannot move | It makes the legs longer | It glues the table to the floor
test: Which of these is a structure built mostly from triangles? | The Eiffel Tower | A tall brick wall | A round snow igloo | A stone pyramid in Egypt
test: why do bridges have all those triangles | A triangle keeps its shape when pushed, so the frame is rigid | Triangles use more steel, so they are heavier | Triangles let the wind blow straight through | Triangles look nice, but add no strength
test: why's there steel rods inside concrete | Concrete is weak in tension, and the steel resists stretching | The steel keeps the concrete warm and stops it cracking in winter | The steel makes the concrete lighter | The steel stops water soaking into it
test: Ava says a flat sheet of paper is just as stiff as a rolled one. true? | No, rolling or folding paper makes it much stiffer | Yes, paper is just as stiff however you shape or fold it | Yes, but only thick paper | No, a flat sheet is stiffer than a rolled one
test: my brother reckons tall towers are hardest to tip if heavy stuff's at the top. right? | No, a wide base and weight low down make things hard to tip | Yes, heavy stuff at the top pushes the tower down and holds it firm | Yes, but only in strong winds | No, weight makes no difference to tipping
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the forces designated compression and tension? | Compression squashes, whereas tension stretches | Compression stretches, whereas tension squashes | Both forces squash an object | Both forces stretch an object
exam: Identify the principal reason for which steel bars are placed within reinforced concrete. | So that the concrete may resist stretching as well as squashing | So that the concrete may be rendered lighter | So that the concrete may dry more quickly | So that the concrete may conduct electricity
exam: It is given that a square frame is readily pushed out of shape. Determine the addition by which it may be rendered rigid. | A diagonal strut dividing it into two triangles | A second square frame placed beside it and joined to it | A heavier weight placed upon its top | A coat of paint upon each side
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the stability of an object. | An object with a wide base and its weight low down is hard to tip over | An object with a narrow base and its weight high up is hardest to tip | The shape of the base has no effect upon stability | Only heavy objects can be stable
